␀set VIMARGS=%%VIMARGS%% %%1
␀␀␀␀shift
␀␀goto loopstart
␀:loopend
␀␀␀Installing .bat files
---------------------
The vim.bat file is written in one of the directories in $PATH.
This makes it possible to start Vim from the command line.
If vim.exe can be found in $PATH, the choice for vim.bat will not be
present.  It is assumed you will use the existing vim.exe.
If vim.bat can already be found in $PATH this is probably for an old
version of Vim (but this is not checked!).  You can overwrite it.
If no vim.bat already exists, you can select one of the directories in
$PATH for creating the batch file, or disable creating a vim.bat file.

If you choose not to create the vim.bat file, Vim can still be executed
in other ways, but not from the command line.

The same applies to choices for gvim, evim, (g)view, and (g)vimdiff.
The first item can be used to change the path for all of them.
␀␀␀␀
␀␀␀Creating a _vimrc file
----------------------
The _vimrc file is used to set options for how Vim behaves.
The install program can create a _vimrc file with a few basic choices.
You can edit this file later to tune your preferences.
If you already have a _vimrc or .vimrc file it can be overwritten.
Don't do that if you have made changes to it.
␀␀␀if .%%OS%%==.Windows_NT goto ntaction
␀␀
␀␀␀Vim features
------------
(this choice is only available when creating a _vimrc file)
1. Vim can run in Vi-compatible mode.  Many nice Vim features are then
   disabled.  Only choose Vi-compatible if you really need full Vi
   compatibility.
2. Vim runs in not-Vi-compatible mode.  Vim is still mostly Vi compatible,
   but adds nice features like multi-level undo.
3. Running Vim with some enhancements is useful when you want some of
   the nice Vim features, but have a slow computer and want to keep it
   really fast.
4. Syntax highlighting shows many files in color.  Not only does this look
   nice, it also makes it easier to spot errors and you can work faster.
   The other features include editing compressed files.
␀Windows key mapping
-------------------
(this choice is only available when creating a _vimrc file)
Under MS-Windows the CTRL-C key copies text to the clipboard and CTRL-V
pastes text from the clipboard.  There are a few more keys like these.
Unfortunately, in Vim these keys normally have another meaning.
1. Choose to have the keys like they normally are in Vim (useful if you
   also use Vim on other systems).
2. Choose to have the keys work like they are used on MS-Windows (useful
   if you mostly work on MS-Windows).
␀␀␀Mouse use
---------
(this choice is only available when creating a _vimrc file)
The right mouse button can be used in two ways:
1. The Unix way is to extend an existing selection.  The popup menu is
   not available.
2. The MS-Windows way is to show a popup menu, which allows you to
   copy/paste text, undo/redo, etc.  Extending the selection can still be
   done by keeping SHIFT pressed while using the left mouse button
␀␀␀if .%%VIMNOFORK%%==.1 goto nofork
␀␀Edit-with-Vim context menu entry
--------------------------------
(this choice is only available when gvim.exe and gvimext.dll are present)
You can associate different file types with Vim, so that you can (double)
click on a file to edit it with Vim.  This means you have to individually
select each file type.
An alternative is the option offered here: Install an "Edit with Vim"
entry in the popup menu for the right mouse button.  This means you can
edit any file with Vim.
␀␀␀start ␀␀"Open With..." context menu entry
--------------------------------
(this choice is only available when gvim.exe is present)
This option adds Vim to the "Open With..." entry in the popup menu for
the right mouse button.  This also makes it possible to edit HTML files
directly from Internet Explorer.
␀␀␀␀"%%VIM_EXE_DIR%%\%s" %s %%VIMARGS%%
␀␀␀␀Add Vim to the Start menu
-------------------------
In Windows 95 and later, Vim can be added to the Start menu.  This will
create a submenu with an entry for vim, gvim, evim, vimdiff, etc..
␀goto eof
␀␀␀
␀␀␀Icons on the desktop
--------------------
(these choices are only available when installing gvim)
In Windows 95 and later, shortcuts (icons) can be created on the Desktop.
␀␀␀␀Create plugin directories
-------------------------
Plugin directories allow extending Vim by dropping a file into a directory.
This choice allows creating them in $HOME (if you have a home directory) or
$VIM (used for everybody on the system).
␀␀␀
␀␀␀:nofork
␀␀␀␀Hit Enter to continue, b (back) or q (quit help): ␀␀start /w ␀␀␀"%%VIM_EXE_DIR%%\%s" %s %%VIMARGS%%
␀␀␀␀goto eof
␀␀␀
␀␀␀:ntaction
␀␀rem for WinNT we can use %%*
␀␀␀
␀␀␀if .%%VIMNOFORK%%==.1 goto noforknt
␀␀␀␀start "dummy" /b ␀␀␀"%%VIM_EXE_DIR%%\%s" %s %%*
␀␀␀␀goto eof
␀␀␀
␀␀␀:noforknt
␀␀start "dummy" /b /wait ␀"%%VIM_EXE_DIR%%\%s" %s %%*
␀␀␀␀
:eof
␀␀set VIMARGS=
␀␀␀set VIMNOFORK=
